ys passed as arguments to a class\'s `__init__`, only checking environment variables.",\n  "failure_reason": "The test imports the `api_keys_required` decorator and defines a class that receives an API key via its `__init__` arguments. It then expects the decorator to consider that key as satisfied. The decorator implementation in the repository only inspects `os.getenv` for the required keys and never looks at the arguments supplied to the class constructor. Consequently the decorator raises a missing‑key error, causing the test assertion to fail. No model calls are made because the repository is run unchanged, so the failure is purely due to the static code behavior. The lack of argument handling in the decorator is the terminal cause of the unsolved test.",\n  "agent_design_issue": "The design flaw resides in the `api_keys_required` decorator implementation (e.g., in `decorators.py` or a similarly named module under `agentissue_program`). It is hard‑coded to validate required keys solely against environment variables (`os.getenv`) and does not inspect the wrapped class\'s `__init__` signature or the values passed at instantiation. This makes the decorator unusable when callers provide keys programmatically. A proper fix would extend the decorator to accept an optional mapping of keys from the class\'s `__init__` arguments (or to inspect `kwargs` for matching key names) before falling back to environment lookup. The change should be made in the decorator\'s source file, adding logic to capture constructor arguments and treat them as valid sources for the required API keys.",\n  "affected_component": "tool_use",\n  "defect_class": "context",\n  "severity": "high",\n  "confidence": "medium"\n}\nJSONEOF\necho COMPLETE_TASK_AND_SUBMIT_FINAL_OUTPUT && cat /tmp/result_camel_1273.json</mswea_bash_command>'}},)
